Use flexbox on the parent:

.parent {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

Grid works too: display: grid; place-items: center;. Avoid absolute positioning unless the layout truly needs it.

Prefer fluid units and flex or grid over fixed pixel widths, then add media queries for genuine breakpoints:

.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}

This reflows from one column to many without any breakpoints at all.

A closure is a function that keeps access to the variables of the scope where it was created, even after that scope has returned.

function counter() {
  let n = 0;
  return () => ++n;
}

const next = counter();
next(); // 1
next(); // 2

Each call to counter() creates an independent n.

async function load(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

Always check res.ok: fetch only rejects on network failures, not on 404 or 500.

A minimal scene with a spinning cube:

import * as THREE from 'three';

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(60, innerWidth / innerHeight, 0.1, 100);
camera.position.z = 3;

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(innerWidth, innerHeight);
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

const cube = new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.BoxGeometry(),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x44aaff })
);
scene.add(cube);
scene.add(new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 3));

function animate(t) {
  cube.rotation.y = t * 0.001;
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
}
requestAnimationFrame(animate);

Track key state, then integrate in the animation loop:

const keys = new Set();
addEventListener('keydown', e => keys.add(e.code));
addEventListener('keyup', e => keys.delete(e.code));

const speed = 3; // units per second
function update(dt) {
  const dir = new THREE.Vector3();
  if (keys.has('KeyW')) dir.z -= 1;
  if (keys.has('KeyS')) dir.z += 1;
  if (keys.has('KeyA')) dir.x -= 1;
  if (keys.has('KeyD')) dir.x += 1;
  if (dir.lengthSq() > 0) dir.normalize().multiplyScalar(speed * dt);
  camera.position.add(dir);
}

Multiply by delta time so motion does not depend on frame rate. getDelta from a THREE.Clock is the usual source of dt.

Use GLTFLoader from the examples:

import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';

const loader = new GLTFLoader();
loader.load('model.glb', gltf => {
  scene.add(gltf.scene);
}, undefined, err => console.error('load failed', err));

The third argument is progress, the fourth is the error handler. Serve the file over http, not file://, or the request will be blocked.

Delta time is the number of seconds since the previous frame. Multiplying speed by it makes motion frame-rate independent:

position += velocity * dt;

Clamp it (for example Math.min(dt, 0.1)) so a tab that was in the background does not teleport objects across the level.

A platformer jump needs a few tuned variables: gravity, jump velocity, and a maximum fall speed.

velocity.y += gravity * dt;
position.y += velocity.y * dt;
if (onGround && jumpPressed) velocity.y = jumpSpeed;

Add variable jump height by cutting velocity.y when the key is released early, and add coyote time (a few frames of forgiveness after leaving a ledge) so the controls feel fair.

A state machine has a current state and explicit transitions:

const states = { menu, playing, paused, gameOver };

Only the active state receives update calls, and transitions are explicit events such as startGame or playerDied. It stops the classic bug where the menu keeps updating while the game is paused.

Compute the direction to the player and move along it, with a small amount of smoothing:

const dx = player.x - enemy.x;
const dy = player.y - enemy.y;
const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
if (dist < detectRange && dist > stopRange) {
  enemy.x += (dx / dist) * speed * dt;
  enemy.y += (dy / dist) * speed * dt;
}

Add a detection radius and a stop radius so enemies do not jitter on top of the player, and separate the speed from the frame time.

Use the Web Audio API or an audio element per effect:

const jump = new Audio('assets/jump.wav');
jump.volume = 0.4;
jump.play();

Browsers block audio until the first user gesture, so create or resume an AudioContext inside a click or keydown handler. Pool short sounds instead of creating a new Audio object for every hit.

Store a small JSON blob in localStorage:

const save = { level: 3, coins: 12, hp: 5 };
localStorage.setItem('save', JSON.stringify(save));

const loaded = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('save') ?? 'null');

Version the blob so future changes can migrate old saves, and validate it on load because the user can edit it.
